<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kokura Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Kokura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain guarding the Kanmon Straits in northern Kyushu, held by the Hosokawa and then by the Ogasawara, and defeated by Choshu in 1866.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadaoki">Hosokawa Tadaoki</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buzen-province">Buzen</a> province and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a> castle from 1602 on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanmon-straits">Kanmon Straits</a>. In 1632 the Hosokawa were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogasawara-tadazane">Ogasawara Tadazane</a>, a relative of the Tokugawa, took over Kokura with 150,000 koku.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogasawara-clan">Ogasawara</a>, a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> family, ruled until the end of the period with a mission to watch over the outside lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and the sea route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> and Kyushu. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a> lay at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> road, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> missions travelling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a> crossed the straits to Honshu.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> in 1866, Choshu forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takasugi-shinsaku">Takasugi Shinsaku</a> crossed the straits; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a> burned its castle and withdrew inland to Kawara. After abolition in 1871 the area became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka-prefecture">Fukuoka Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kokura-domain.jpg" alt="Kokura Domain" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Historian · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kokura_castle_from_the_Japanese_garden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kokura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hosokawa Tadaoki, Ogasawara Tadazane</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kokura han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=kokura-domain" aria-label="Kokura Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8017 949 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kokura Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8337 1205L8530 1145M8337 1205L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8337 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8337 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kokura. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=kokura-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
